WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:09.976 --> 00:12.036 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

00:15.876 --> 00:20.636 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:21.476 --> 00:25.536 align:middle
Earlier today, the Federal
Open Market Committee decided

00:25.586 --> 00:28.826 align:middle
to raise the target range
for the federal funds rate

00:29.366 --> 00:31.386 align:middle
by 1/4 percentage point,

00:31.806 --> 00:34.786 align:middle
bringing it to 1/4
to 1/2 percent.

00:35.886 --> 00:38.006 align:middle
This action marks the end

00:38.066 --> 00:41.406 align:middle
of an extraordinary
seven-year period during

00:41.406 --> 00:44.936 align:middle
which the federal funds
rate was held near zero

00:45.346 --> 00:47.706 align:middle
to support the recovery
of the economy

00:48.146 --> 00:50.926 align:middle
from the worst financial
crisis and recession

00:50.926 --> 00:52.336 align:middle
since the Great Depression.

00:53.296 --> 00:56.496 align:middle
It also recognizes the
considerable progress

00:56.496 --> 01:01.296 align:middle
that has been made toward
restoring jobs, raising incomes,

01:01.616 --> 01:05.706 align:middle
and easing the economic hardship
of millions of Americans.

01:06.116 --> 01:09.426 align:middle
And it reflects the
Committee's confidence

01:09.426 --> 01:12.016 align:middle
that the economy will
continue to strengthen.

01:13.046 --> 01:16.616 align:middle
The economic recovery has
clearly come a long way,

01:16.986 --> 01:18.846 align:middle
although it is not yet complete.

01:19.746 --> 01:23.286 align:middle
Room for further improvement
in the labor market remains,

01:23.666 --> 01:28.176 align:middle
and inflation continues to run
below our longer-run objective.

01:29.036 --> 01:33.606 align:middle
But with the economy performing
well and expected to continue

01:33.606 --> 01:37.436 align:middle
to do so, the Committee
judged that a modest increase

01:37.436 --> 01:40.956 align:middle
in the federal funds rate
target is now appropriate,

01:41.596 --> 01:45.166 align:middle
recognizing that even
after this increase,

01:45.166 --> 01:47.776 align:middle
monetary policy remains
accommodative.

01:48.756 --> 01:50.636 align:middle
As I will explain, the process

01:50.636 --> 01:54.986 align:middle
of normalizing interest rates
is likely to proceed gradually,

01:55.646 --> 01:59.086 align:middle
although future policy
actions will obviously depend

01:59.476 --> 02:03.556 align:middle
on how the economy evolves
relative to our objectives

02:03.746 --> 02:07.006 align:middle
of maximum employment
and 2 percent inflation.

02:08.116 --> 02:10.556 align:middle
Since March, the
Committee has stated

02:10.556 --> 02:14.026 align:middle
that it would raise the target
range for the federal funds rate

02:14.536 --> 02:17.546 align:middle
when it had seen further
improvement in the labor market

02:17.976 --> 02:21.836 align:middle
and was reasonably confident
that inflation would move back

02:21.906 --> 02:25.436 align:middle
to its 2 percent objective
over the medium term.

02:26.356 --> 02:29.296 align:middle
In our judgment, these
two criteria have now

02:29.346 --> 02:30.366 align:middle
been satisfied.

02:32.046 --> 02:35.156 align:middle
The labor market has clearly
shown significant further

02:35.156 --> 02:38.736 align:middle
improvement toward our
objective of maximum employment.

02:39.756 --> 02:44.446 align:middle
So far this year, a total of
2.3 million jobs have been added

02:44.496 --> 02:48.196 align:middle
to the economy, and over the
most recent three months,

02:48.496 --> 02:53.576 align:middle
job gains have averaged an
estimated 218,000 per month,

02:54.226 --> 02:57.376 align:middle
similar to the average pace
since the beginning of the year.

02:58.356 --> 03:01.346 align:middle
The unemployment rate,
at 5 percent in November,

03:01.686 --> 03:06.086 align:middle
is down 0.6 percentage point
from the end of last year

03:06.646 --> 03:11.406 align:middle
and is close to the median of
FOMC participants' estimates

03:11.596 --> 03:13.456 align:middle
of its longer-run normal level.

03:14.396 --> 03:16.376 align:middle
A broader measure
of unemployment

03:16.376 --> 03:20.546 align:middle
that includes individuals who
want and are available to work

03:21.106 --> 03:24.696 align:middle
but have not actively
searched recently and people

03:24.696 --> 03:26.086 align:middle
who are working part time

03:26.086 --> 03:29.706 align:middle
but would rather work
full time also has shown

03:29.706 --> 03:30.956 align:middle
solid improvement.

03:31.856 --> 03:35.526 align:middle
That said, some cyclical
weakness likely remains:

03:36.236 --> 03:39.746 align:middle
The labor force participation
rate is still below estimates

03:39.956 --> 03:41.606 align:middle
of its demographic trend,

03:42.346 --> 03:46.666 align:middle
involuntary part-time employment
remains somewhat elevated,

03:47.336 --> 03:50.596 align:middle
and wage growth has yet to
show a sustained pickup.

03:51.856 --> 03:55.246 align:middle
The improvement in employment
conditions this year has

03:55.246 --> 03:59.166 align:middle
occurred amid continued
expansion in economic activity.

04:00.196 --> 04:04.826 align:middle
U.S. real gross domestic product
is estimated to have increased

04:04.826 --> 04:08.086 align:middle
at an average pace
of 2-1/4 percent

04:08.086 --> 04:10.696 align:middle
over the first three
quarters of the year.

04:11.886 --> 04:16.406 align:middle
Net exports have been restrained
by subdued foreign growth

04:16.406 --> 04:18.376 align:middle
and the appreciation
of the dollar,

04:19.126 --> 04:21.236 align:middle
but this weakness
has been offset

04:21.326 --> 04:24.746 align:middle
by solid expansion
of domestic spending.

04:25.706 --> 04:27.866 align:middle
Continued job gains
and increases

04:27.866 --> 04:31.766 align:middle
in real disposable income have
supported household spending,

04:32.216 --> 04:34.816 align:middle
and purchases of new
motor vehicles have been

04:34.816 --> 04:36.166 align:middle
particularly strong.

04:37.136 --> 04:40.226 align:middle
Residential investment has
been rising at a faster pace

04:40.226 --> 04:42.766 align:middle
than last year, although
the level

04:42.766 --> 04:45.276 align:middle
of new homebuilding
still remains low.

04:46.416 --> 04:48.986 align:middle
And outside of the
drilling and mining sector,

04:49.256 --> 04:52.586 align:middle
where lower oil prices have
led to substantial cuts

04:52.886 --> 04:56.726 align:middle
in investment outlays,
business investment has posted

04:56.726 --> 04:57.756 align:middle
solid gains.

04:58.836 --> 05:03.256 align:middle
The Committee currently expects
that, with gradual adjustments

05:03.256 --> 05:05.386 align:middle
in the stance of
monetary policy,

05:06.296 --> 05:10.306 align:middle
economic activity will continue
to expand at a moderate pace

05:10.796 --> 05:14.196 align:middle
and labor market indicators
will continue to strengthen.

05:15.256 --> 05:18.166 align:middle
Although developments
abroad still pose risks

05:18.196 --> 05:21.806 align:middle
to U.S. economic growth,
these risks appear

05:21.806 --> 05:23.896 align:middle
to have lessened
since last summer.

05:25.126 --> 05:28.216 align:middle
Overall, the Committee sees
the risks to the outlook

05:28.216 --> 05:32.386 align:middle
for both economic activity and
the labor market as balanced.

05:32.596 --> 05:36.816 align:middle
The anticipation of
ongoing economic growth

05:37.316 --> 05:38.746 align:middle
and additional improvement

05:38.746 --> 05:43.466 align:middle
in labor market conditions is
an important factor underpinning

05:43.466 --> 05:47.156 align:middle
the Committee's confidence
that inflation will return

05:47.156 --> 05:50.036 align:middle
to our 2 percent objective
over the medium term.

05:51.106 --> 05:53.576 align:middle
Overall consumer
price inflation--

05:54.036 --> 05:55.806 align:middle
as measured by the price index

05:55.806 --> 05:58.136 align:middle
for personal consumption
expenditures--

05:58.636 --> 06:03.446 align:middle
was only 1/4 percent over the
12 months ending in October.

06:04.486 --> 06:06.236 align:middle
However, much of the shortfall

06:06.236 --> 06:10.436 align:middle
from our 2 percent objective
reflected the sharp declines

06:10.436 --> 06:13.856 align:middle
in energy prices since
the middle of last year,

06:14.566 --> 06:17.656 align:middle
and the effects of these
declines should dissipate

06:17.656 --> 06:18.446 align:middle
over time.

06:19.166 --> 06:23.316 align:middle
The appreciation of the dollar
has also weighed on inflation

06:23.756 --> 06:25.906 align:middle
by holding down import prices.

06:27.296 --> 06:30.026 align:middle
As these transitory
influences fade

06:30.636 --> 06:33.066 align:middle
and as the labor market
strengthens further,

06:33.566 --> 06:35.916 align:middle
the Committee expects
inflation to rise

06:35.916 --> 06:38.216 align:middle
to 2 percent over
the medium term.

06:38.986 --> 06:40.936 align:middle
The Committee's confidence

06:40.936 --> 06:45.166 align:middle
in the inflation outlook rests
importantly on its judgment

06:45.566 --> 06:48.586 align:middle
that longer-run inflation
expectations remain

06:48.586 --> 06:49.306 align:middle
well anchored.

06:50.376 --> 06:53.436 align:middle
In this regard, although
some survey measures

06:53.436 --> 06:57.026 align:middle
of longer-run inflation
expectations have edged down,

06:57.676 --> 07:00.146 align:middle
overall they have been
reasonably stable.

07:01.246 --> 07:02.506 align:middle
Market-based measures

07:02.506 --> 07:07.696 align:middle
of inflation compensation remain
near historically low levels,

07:08.396 --> 07:11.386 align:middle
although the declines in these
measures over the past year

07:11.386 --> 07:14.576 align:middle
and a half may reflect
changes in risk

07:14.576 --> 07:19.126 align:middle
and liquidity premiums rather
than an outright decline

07:19.126 --> 07:21.006 align:middle
in inflation expectations.

07:22.156 --> 07:24.216 align:middle
Our statement emphasizes that,

07:24.216 --> 07:27.046 align:middle
in considering future
policy decisions,

07:27.266 --> 07:29.696 align:middle
we will carefully monitor actual

07:29.696 --> 07:33.136 align:middle
and expected progress
toward our inflation goal.

07:33.946 --> 07:37.486 align:middle
This general assessment of
the outlook is reflected

07:37.486 --> 07:41.116 align:middle
in the individual economic
projections submitted

07:41.116 --> 07:43.956 align:middle
for this meeting by
FOMC participants.

07:44.806 --> 07:49.526 align:middle
As always, each participant's
projections are conditioned

07:49.736 --> 07:54.246 align:middle
on his or her own view of
appropriate monetary policy.

07:55.066 --> 07:56.786 align:middle
Participants' projections

07:56.786 --> 07:59.836 align:middle
for real GDP growth
are little changed

07:59.916 --> 08:02.746 align:middle
from the projections
made in conjunction

08:02.746 --> 08:05.126 align:middle
with the September FOMC meeting.

08:05.796 --> 08:10.356 align:middle
The median projection for
real GDP growth is 2.1 percent

08:10.556 --> 08:15.266 align:middle
for this year and rises
to 2.4 percent in 2016,

08:16.036 --> 08:17.996 align:middle
somewhat above the
median estimate

08:17.996 --> 08:20.236 align:middle
of the longer-run
normal growth rate.

08:21.086 --> 08:25.556 align:middle
Thereafter, the median growth
projection declines toward its

08:25.556 --> 08:26.826 align:middle
longer-run rate.

08:27.986 --> 08:30.406 align:middle
The median projection
for the unemployment rate

08:30.406 --> 08:34.036 align:middle
in the fourth quarter of this
year stands at 5 percent,

08:34.696 --> 08:36.296 align:middle
close to the median estimate

08:36.296 --> 08:38.726 align:middle
of the longer-run
normal unemployment rate.

08:39.456 --> 08:43.076 align:middle
Committee participants generally
see the unemployment rate

08:43.136 --> 08:47.086 align:middle
declining a little further next
year and then leveling out.

08:47.576 --> 08:51.876 align:middle
The path of the median
unemployment rate is slightly

08:51.876 --> 08:53.336 align:middle
lower than in September,

08:54.196 --> 08:57.836 align:middle
and while the median longer-run
normal unemployment rate has not

08:57.886 --> 09:02.216 align:middle
changed, some participants
edged down their estimates.

09:03.196 --> 09:06.796 align:middle
Finally, FOMC participants
project inflation

09:07.006 --> 09:11.436 align:middle
to be very low this year,
largely reflecting lower prices

09:11.436 --> 09:14.146 align:middle
for energy and non-energy
imports.

09:15.056 --> 09:18.766 align:middle
As the transitory factors
holding down inflation abate

09:19.296 --> 09:23.496 align:middle
and labor market conditions
continue to strengthen,

09:24.136 --> 09:27.346 align:middle
the median inflation
projection rises

09:27.636 --> 09:32.976 align:middle
from just 0.4 percent this
year to 1.6 percent next year

09:33.466 --> 09:39.286 align:middle
and reaches 1.9 percent in
2017 and 2 percent in 2018.

09:40.016 --> 09:44.156 align:middle
The path of the median inflation
projections is little changed

09:44.156 --> 09:45.036 align:middle
from September.

09:46.536 --> 09:49.496 align:middle
With inflation currently
still low,

09:50.076 --> 09:53.416 align:middle
why is the Committee raising
the federal funds rate target?

09:54.036 --> 09:57.566 align:middle
As I have already noted,
much of the recent softness

09:57.566 --> 10:00.636 align:middle
in inflation is due
to transitory factors

10:01.026 --> 10:05.496 align:middle
that we expect to abate over
time, and diminishing slack

10:05.496 --> 10:09.666 align:middle
in labor and product markets
should put upward pressure

10:09.916 --> 10:11.326 align:middle
on inflation as well.

10:12.436 --> 10:15.496 align:middle
In addition, we recognize
that it takes time

10:15.496 --> 10:20.526 align:middle
for monetary policy actions to
affect future economic outcomes.

10:21.576 --> 10:25.666 align:middle
Were the FOMC to delay the
start of policy normalization

10:25.926 --> 10:29.046 align:middle
for too long, we would
likely end up having

10:29.046 --> 10:33.216 align:middle
to tighten policy relatively
abruptly at some point

10:33.546 --> 10:37.236 align:middle
to keep the economy from
overheating and inflation

10:37.236 --> 10:40.086 align:middle
from significantly
overshooting our objective.

10:40.916 --> 10:44.056 align:middle
Such an abrupt tightening
could increase the risk

10:44.056 --> 10:46.376 align:middle
of pushing the economy
into recession.

10:46.856 --> 10:49.916 align:middle
As I have often noted,
the importance

10:49.916 --> 10:52.646 align:middle
of our initial increase
in the target range

10:52.646 --> 10:56.196 align:middle
for the federal funds rate
should not be overstated:

10:57.026 --> 10:58.826 align:middle
Even after today's increase,

10:58.826 --> 11:02.416 align:middle
the stance of monetary
policy remains accommodative,

11:02.976 --> 11:05.206 align:middle
thereby supporting
further improvement

11:05.206 --> 11:09.726 align:middle
in labor market conditions and
a return to 2 percent inflation.

11:10.896 --> 11:12.806 align:middle
As we indicated in
our statement,

11:13.156 --> 11:17.076 align:middle
"the Committee expects that
economic conditions will evolve

11:17.076 --> 11:21.136 align:middle
in a manner that will
warrant only gradual increases

11:21.136 --> 11:25.046 align:middle
in the federal funds rate; the
federal funds rate is likely

11:25.086 --> 11:29.646 align:middle
to remain, for some time,
below levels that are expected

11:29.696 --> 11:31.486 align:middle
to prevail in the longer run."

11:32.126 --> 11:35.876 align:middle
This expectation is
consistent with the view

11:36.416 --> 11:39.216 align:middle
that the neutral nominal
federal funds rate--

11:39.976 --> 11:42.796 align:middle
defined as the value of
the federal funds rate

11:43.276 --> 11:47.446 align:middle
that would be neither
expansionary nor contractionary

11:47.836 --> 11:50.376 align:middle
if the economy were
operating near potential--

11:51.446 --> 11:55.966 align:middle
is currently low by historical
standards and is likely

11:55.966 --> 11:58.546 align:middle
to rise only gradually
over time.

11:59.926 --> 12:05.136 align:middle
One indication that the neutral
funds rate is unusually low is

12:05.136 --> 12:08.786 align:middle
that U.S. economic growth
has been only moderate

12:08.786 --> 12:11.996 align:middle
in recent years despite
the very low level

12:11.996 --> 12:13.416 align:middle
of the federal funds rate

12:13.866 --> 12:16.716 align:middle
and the Federal Reserve's
very large holdings

12:17.086 --> 12:19.006 align:middle
of longer-term securities.

12:19.456 --> 12:22.306 align:middle
Had the neutral rate
been running closer

12:22.356 --> 12:24.056 align:middle
to its longer-run level,

12:24.636 --> 12:27.616 align:middle
these policy actions
would have been expected

12:27.996 --> 12:31.706 align:middle
to foster a much more
rapid economic expansion.

12:32.816 --> 12:34.126 align:middle
The marked decline

12:34.126 --> 12:37.586 align:middle
in the neutral federal
funds rate may be partially

12:37.586 --> 12:42.056 align:middle
attributable to a range of
persistent economic headwinds

12:42.316 --> 12:44.546 align:middle
that have weighed
on aggregate demand.

12:45.406 --> 12:47.456 align:middle
Following the financial crisis,

12:47.736 --> 12:51.626 align:middle
these headwinds included
tighter underwriting standards

12:51.896 --> 12:57.206 align:middle
and limited access to credit
for some borrowers, deleveraging

12:57.206 --> 13:00.166 align:middle
by many households to
reduce debt burdens,

13:00.796 --> 13:05.726 align:middle
contractionary fiscal policy,
weak growth abroad coupled

13:05.726 --> 13:08.536 align:middle
with a significant
appreciation of the dollar,

13:09.296 --> 13:11.926 align:middle
slower productivity
and labor force growth,

13:12.666 --> 13:15.896 align:middle
and elevated uncertainty
about the economic outlook.

13:16.676 --> 13:19.326 align:middle
Although the restraint
imposed by many

13:19.376 --> 13:22.006 align:middle
of these factors has
declined noticeably

13:22.316 --> 13:24.936 align:middle
over the past few years, some

13:24.936 --> 13:27.226 align:middle
of these effects have
remained significant.

13:28.226 --> 13:29.896 align:middle
As these effects abate,

13:30.476 --> 13:33.836 align:middle
the neutral federal funds rate
should gradually move higher

13:33.836 --> 13:34.696 align:middle
over time.

13:35.756 --> 13:38.266 align:middle
This view is implicitly
reflected

13:38.266 --> 13:42.936 align:middle
in participants' projections
of appropriate monetary policy.

13:43.856 --> 13:47.836 align:middle
The median projection for
the federal funds rate rises

13:47.866 --> 13:52.786 align:middle
gradually to nearly 1-1/2
percent in late 2016

13:53.286 --> 13:56.296 align:middle
and 2-1/2 percent in late 2017.

13:57.356 --> 14:01.266 align:middle
As the factors restraining
economic growth continue to fade

14:01.266 --> 14:06.656 align:middle
over time, the median rate rises
to 3-1/4 percent by the end

14:06.656 --> 14:11.076 align:middle
of 2018, close to its
longer-run normal level.

14:12.226 --> 14:15.096 align:middle
Compared with the
projections made in September,

14:15.596 --> 14:18.976 align:middle
a number of participants
lowered somewhat their paths

14:18.976 --> 14:22.056 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate, although changes

14:22.056 --> 14:24.346 align:middle
to the median path
are fairly minor.

14:24.576 --> 14:28.426 align:middle
I'd like to underscore
that the forecasts

14:28.426 --> 14:31.966 align:middle
of the appropriate path of the
federal funds rate, as usual,

14:32.456 --> 14:36.376 align:middle
are conditional on participants'
individual projections

14:36.846 --> 14:40.806 align:middle
of the most likely outcomes
for economic growth, employment

14:40.806 --> 14:43.146 align:middle
and inflation, and
other factors.

14:44.096 --> 14:48.826 align:middle
However, the actual path of the
federal funds rate will depend

14:48.826 --> 14:53.036 align:middle
on the economic outlook as
informed by incoming data.

14:54.116 --> 14:58.326 align:middle
Stronger growth or a more
rapid increase in inflation

14:58.326 --> 15:01.606 align:middle
than we currently
anticipate would suggest

15:01.606 --> 15:05.636 align:middle
that the neutral federal funds
rate was rising more quickly

15:05.686 --> 15:08.586 align:middle
than expected, making
it appropriate

15:08.586 --> 15:12.056 align:middle
to raise the federal funds
rate more quickly as well.

15:13.146 --> 15:16.166 align:middle
Conversely, if the
economy were to disappoint,

15:16.906 --> 15:20.256 align:middle
the federal funds rate would
likely rise more slowly.

15:21.516 --> 15:25.736 align:middle
The Committee will continue its
policy of reinvesting proceeds

15:25.736 --> 15:28.526 align:middle
from maturing Treasury
securities

15:28.856 --> 15:31.966 align:middle
and principal payments
from agency debt

15:31.966 --> 15:33.756 align:middle
and mortgage-backed securities.

15:34.606 --> 15:36.966 align:middle
As highlighted in
our policy statement,

15:37.396 --> 15:41.956 align:middle
we anticipate continuing this
policy "until normalization

15:42.216 --> 15:46.256 align:middle
of the level of the federal
funds rate is well under way."

15:47.206 --> 15:49.526 align:middle
Maintaining our sizable holdings

15:49.756 --> 15:53.886 align:middle
of longer-term securities should
help maintain accommodative

15:53.886 --> 15:57.946 align:middle
financial conditions and
should reduce the risk

15:58.176 --> 16:01.036 align:middle
that the federal funds
rate might return

16:01.036 --> 16:03.546 align:middle
to the effective lower
bound in the event

16:03.756 --> 16:05.546 align:middle
of future adverse shocks.

16:06.656 --> 16:10.046 align:middle
Finally, in conjunction
with our policy statement,

16:10.356 --> 16:13.096 align:middle
we also released an
implementation note

16:13.426 --> 16:16.736 align:middle
that provides details on
the tools that we are using

16:17.126 --> 16:20.646 align:middle
to raise the federal funds
rate into the new target range.

16:21.566 --> 16:22.876 align:middle
Specifically, the Board

16:22.876 --> 16:26.696 align:middle
of Governors raised the
interest rate paid on required

16:27.006 --> 16:30.196 align:middle
and excess reserves
to 1/2 percent,

16:31.106 --> 16:35.226 align:middle
and the FOMC authorized
overnight reverse repurchase

16:35.226 --> 16:38.996 align:middle
operations at an offering
rate of 1/4 percent.

16:39.976 --> 16:42.916 align:middle
Both of these changes will
be effective tomorrow.

16:43.826 --> 16:47.756 align:middle
To ensure sufficient
monetary control at the onset

16:47.756 --> 16:52.476 align:middle
of the normalization process,
we have, for the time being,

16:52.966 --> 16:55.106 align:middle
suspended the aggregate cap

16:55.106 --> 16:58.946 align:middle
on overnight reverse repurchase
transactions that has been

16:58.946 --> 17:02.826 align:middle
in place during the testing
phase of this facility.

17:03.466 --> 17:05.956 align:middle
Recall that the Committee
intends to phase

17:05.956 --> 17:09.216 align:middle
out this facility when
it is no longer needed

17:09.536 --> 17:11.746 align:middle
to help control the
federal funds rate.

17:12.046 --> 17:17.476 align:middle
The Board of Governors also
approved a 1/4 percentage point

17:17.476 --> 17:22.096 align:middle
increase in the discount rate
for primary credit to 1 percent.

17:23.526 --> 17:27.006 align:middle
Based on the extensive
testing of our policy tools

17:27.006 --> 17:30.506 align:middle
in recent years, the
Committee is confident

17:30.866 --> 17:34.736 align:middle
that the normalization
process will proceed smoothly.

17:35.486 --> 17:39.496 align:middle
Nonetheless, as part of
prudent contingency planning,

17:39.496 --> 17:43.596 align:middle
we will be monitoring financial
market developments closely

17:43.596 --> 17:47.196 align:middle
in the coming days and are
prepared to make adjustments

17:47.196 --> 17:50.256 align:middle
to our tools if that
proves necessary

17:50.536 --> 17:54.526 align:middle
to maintain appropriate
control over money market rates.

17:55.286 --> 17:55.986 align:middle
Thank you.

17:56.326 --> 17:58.456 align:middle
I will be happy to
take your questions.

17:59.536 --> 18:02.386 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

18:04.126 --> 18:06.376 align:middle
Marty Crutsinger with
the Associated Press.

18:06.906 --> 18:10.036 align:middle
I guess the word is "finally."

18:10.656 --> 18:15.176 align:middle
The-- we have asked you for so
long, "Why were you delaying?

18:15.216 --> 18:16.066 align:middle
Why were you delaying?"

18:16.066 --> 18:20.756 align:middle
So, I'll ask, given developments
around the world that still--

18:20.756 --> 18:26.326 align:middle
there is still weakness, and the
inflation is still nowhere near

18:26.326 --> 18:27.026 align:middle
your target.

18:27.316 --> 18:28.866 align:middle
What made you say "Do it now"?

18:28.866 --> 18:31.466 align:middle
Some have said it was
because you feared a lack

18:31.466 --> 18:33.116 align:middle
of credibility if
you didn't move.

18:33.466 --> 18:35.186 align:middle
Did that play a role
in your decision?

18:35.826 --> 18:36.446 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

18:36.536 --> 18:44.216 align:middle
We decided to move at this time
because we feel the conditions

18:44.216 --> 18:48.806 align:middle
that we set out for a move--

18:49.166 --> 18:52.306 align:middle
namely, further improvement
in the labor market

18:52.346 --> 18:57.056 align:middle
and reasonable confidence
that inflation would move back

18:57.136 --> 18:59.536 align:middle
to 2 percent over
the medium term--

19:00.026 --> 19:03.796 align:middle
we felt that these
conditions had been satisfied.

19:05.556 --> 19:09.496 align:middle
We have been concerned, as
you know, about the risks

19:09.496 --> 19:13.756 align:middle
from the global economy,
and those risks persist,

19:14.126 --> 19:17.616 align:middle
but the U.S. economy has
shown considerable strength.

19:18.346 --> 19:21.846 align:middle
Domestic spending that
accounts for 85 percent

19:22.276 --> 19:23.926 align:middle
of aggregate spending

19:23.926 --> 19:28.826 align:middle
in the U.S. economy has
continued to hold up.

19:28.876 --> 19:30.976 align:middle
It's grown at a solid pace.

19:31.306 --> 19:35.426 align:middle
And while there is a
drag from net exports,

19:35.456 --> 19:39.756 align:middle
from relatively weak growth
abroad, and the appreciation

19:39.756 --> 19:45.086 align:middle
of the dollar, overall, we
decided today that the risks

19:45.086 --> 19:47.046 align:middle
to the outlook for
the labor market

19:47.046 --> 19:49.806 align:middle
and the economy are balanced.

19:50.166 --> 19:55.736 align:middle
And we recognize that monetary
policy operates with lags.

19:57.066 --> 20:01.376 align:middle
We would like to be able
to move in a prudent and,

20:01.376 --> 20:03.866 align:middle
as we've emphasized,
gradual manner.

20:04.166 --> 20:07.746 align:middle
It's been a long time since
the Federal Reserve has raised

20:07.746 --> 20:11.826 align:middle
interest rates, and I think
it's prudent to be able

20:11.826 --> 20:15.956 align:middle
to watch what the impact
is on financial conditions

20:15.956 --> 20:18.336 align:middle
and spending in the economy,

20:18.696 --> 20:24.096 align:middle
and moving in a timely
fashion enables us to do this.

20:24.156 --> 20:26.476 align:middle
Again, I think it's
important not

20:26.566 --> 20:30.396 align:middle
to overblow the significance
of this first move.

20:30.396 --> 20:33.266 align:middle
It's only 25 basis points.

20:33.306 --> 20:36.706 align:middle
It-- monetary policy
remains accommodative.

20:37.706 --> 20:41.576 align:middle
We have indicated that we will
be watching what happens very

20:41.576 --> 20:46.936 align:middle
carefully in the economy
in terms of our actual

20:46.936 --> 20:51.496 align:middle
and forecast-- our
projected conditions relative

20:51.496 --> 20:56.066 align:middle
to our employment and inflation
goals and will adjust policy

20:56.066 --> 21:00.046 align:middle
over time as seems appropriate
to achieve those goals.

21:00.436 --> 21:03.756 align:middle
Our expectation,
as I've indicated,

21:03.756 --> 21:08.616 align:middle
is that policy adjustments
will be gradual over time, but,

21:08.846 --> 21:12.206 align:middle
of course, they will be
informed by the outlook,

21:12.206 --> 21:15.706 align:middle
which in turn will
evolve with incoming data.

21:16.976 --> 21:18.286 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

21:20.096 --> 21:21.496 align:middle
Madam Chair, thank you.

21:21.496 --> 21:23.186 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

21:23.356 --> 21:26.036 align:middle
Under the old regime, before
you were raising rates,

21:26.036 --> 21:27.076 align:middle
it was easy to understand

21:27.406 --> 21:28.986 align:middle
within your mandate
what you wanted to do.

21:28.986 --> 21:31.756 align:middle
You wanted the unemployment rate
to fall, you wanted inflation

21:31.756 --> 21:34.736 align:middle
to rise, and it was easy for
the public to judge the success

21:34.736 --> 21:36.056 align:middle
or failure of your policy.

21:36.786 --> 21:39.696 align:middle
Could you explain under the new
regime what you're looking for?

21:39.696 --> 21:42.466 align:middle
Do you want the unemployment
rate to stop falling?

21:42.466 --> 21:43.816 align:middle
Do you want it to rise?

21:44.266 --> 21:46.156 align:middle
And what is it you hope
for from inflation,

21:46.536 --> 21:48.386 align:middle
which I think is a little
more understandable,

21:48.866 --> 21:52.316 align:middle
or is "neutral" itself
now a policy goal?

21:53.476 --> 21:54.086 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

21:54.176 --> 21:56.276 align:middle
"Neutral" is not a policy goal.

21:56.276 --> 21:57.736 align:middle
It is an assessment.

21:57.736 --> 22:01.146 align:middle
It's a benchmark that
I think is usual--

22:01.256 --> 22:05.496 align:middle
is useful for assessing
the stance of policy.

22:05.876 --> 22:09.746 align:middle
Neutral is essentially
a stance of policy,

22:09.746 --> 22:12.876 align:middle
a level of short-term
rates, which,

22:12.876 --> 22:16.586 align:middle
if the economy were
operating near its potential--

22:16.586 --> 22:20.566 align:middle
and we're reasonably not quite
at that, but reasonably close

22:20.566 --> 22:23.456 align:middle
to it-- it would be a
level that would maintain

22:23.456 --> 22:25.916 align:middle
or sustain those conditions.

22:26.316 --> 22:30.946 align:middle
So, at this point, policy we
judge to be accommodative.

22:31.386 --> 22:38.206 align:middle
The Committee forecasts that the
unemployment rate will continue

22:38.206 --> 22:42.056 align:middle
to decline, and I
think that's important

22:42.056 --> 22:44.006 align:middle
and appropriate for two reasons.

22:44.256 --> 22:48.726 align:middle
First of all, as I've
indicated, I continue to judge

22:48.726 --> 22:52.746 align:middle
that there remains slack in
the economy, margins of slack

22:52.746 --> 22:57.066 align:middle
that are not reflected in the
standard unemployment rate, and,

22:57.246 --> 23:01.116 align:middle
in particular, I've pointed
to the depressed level

23:01.116 --> 23:03.186 align:middle
of labor force participation

23:03.496 --> 23:07.066 align:middle
and also the somewhat
abnormally high level

23:07.066 --> 23:08.536 align:middle
of part-time employment.

23:08.816 --> 23:12.206 align:middle
So a further decline in
the unemployment rate

23:12.206 --> 23:16.126 align:middle
and strengthening of labor
market conditions will help

23:16.126 --> 23:18.406 align:middle
to erode those margins of slack.

23:18.706 --> 23:22.136 align:middle
But also we want to see
inflation move up back

23:22.136 --> 23:25.396 align:middle
to our 2 percent objective
over the medium term,

23:25.666 --> 23:32.226 align:middle
and so seeing above-trend growth
and continuing tightness--

23:32.226 --> 23:35.256 align:middle
greater tightness in labor
and product markets--

23:35.576 --> 23:39.176 align:middle
I think that will help us
achieve our objective as well

23:39.366 --> 23:40.776 align:middle
with respect to inflation.

23:41.466 --> 23:41.986 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

23:42.626 --> 23:45.806 align:middle
Just to follow up, how does
raising rates help get you

23:45.806 --> 23:47.026 align:middle
to either of those goals?

23:47.846 --> 23:48.536 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

23:49.716 --> 23:53.086 align:middle
We have kept rates at
an extremely low level

23:53.086 --> 23:58.446 align:middle
and had a high balance
sheet for a very long time.

23:58.446 --> 24:05.516 align:middle
We have considered the risks
to the outlook and worried

24:05.516 --> 24:08.876 align:middle
about the fact that with
interest rates at zero,

24:08.876 --> 24:13.266 align:middle
we have less scope to
respond to negative shocks

24:13.716 --> 24:16.696 align:middle
than to positive
shocks that would call

24:16.696 --> 24:18.536 align:middle
for a tightening of policy.

24:18.996 --> 24:25.596 align:middle
That is a factor that has
induced us to hold rates

24:25.596 --> 24:27.246 align:middle
at zero for this long.

24:27.716 --> 24:32.466 align:middle
But we recognize that
policy is accommodative,

24:32.946 --> 24:37.646 align:middle
and if we do not begin to
slightly reduce the amount

24:37.646 --> 24:42.686 align:middle
of accommodation, the odds are
good that the economy would end

24:42.686 --> 24:45.336 align:middle
up overshooting both
our employment

24:45.336 --> 24:47.016 align:middle
and inflation objectives.

24:47.446 --> 24:52.196 align:middle
What I-- we would like
to avoid is a situation

24:52.196 --> 24:55.526 align:middle
where we have waited so
long that we're forced

24:55.566 --> 24:57.646 align:middle
to tighten policy abruptly,

24:57.956 --> 25:02.886 align:middle
which risks aborting
what I would like to see

25:02.886 --> 25:07.636 align:middle
as a very long-running
and sustainable expansion.

25:08.136 --> 25:13.696 align:middle
So to keep the economy moving
along the growth path it's

25:13.696 --> 25:19.446 align:middle
on with improving and solid
conditions in labor markets,

25:19.796 --> 25:23.586 align:middle
we would like to avoid a
situation where we have left

25:23.586 --> 25:27.216 align:middle
so much accommodation
in place for so long

25:27.486 --> 25:30.536 align:middle
that we overshoot these
objectives and then have

25:30.536 --> 25:33.686 align:middle
to tighten abruptly
and risk damaging--

25:33.936 --> 25:35.566 align:middle
damaging that performance.

25:36.096 --> 25:38.426 align:middle
JON HILSENRATH.

25:39.136 --> 25:42.836 align:middle
Chair Yellen, Jon Hilsenrath
from the Wall Street Journal.

25:42.836 --> 25:45.356 align:middle
In the sentence in
your statement

25:45.356 --> 25:50.026 align:middle
about gradual increases, in
that section, the Committee says

25:50.026 --> 25:53.266 align:middle
that it "will carefully
monitor" progress--

25:53.616 --> 25:55.456 align:middle
actual and expected
progress on inflation.

25:55.456 --> 25:57.876 align:middle
That's going to read like
some kind of code to a lot

25:57.876 --> 25:58.736 align:middle
of people on Wall Street.

25:58.736 --> 26:00.866 align:middle
Can you describe--
what do you mean

26:00.866 --> 26:03.146 align:middle
when you say "carefully
monitor"?

26:03.436 --> 26:07.086 align:middle
And, specifically, with
regard to what you do next,

26:07.086 --> 26:10.566 align:middle
do you need to see inflation
actually rise at this point

26:11.156 --> 26:13.536 align:middle
in order to raise
interest rates again?

26:13.826 --> 26:14.586 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

26:14.586 --> 26:16.756 align:middle
Well, we recognize

26:17.156 --> 26:21.266 align:middle
that inflation is well
below our 2 percent goal.

26:22.086 --> 26:25.396 align:middle
The entire Committee
is committed

26:25.446 --> 26:29.046 align:middle
to achieving our 2
percent inflation objective

26:29.316 --> 26:35.686 align:middle
over the medium term, just
as we want to make sure

26:36.036 --> 26:39.976 align:middle
that inflation doesn't
persist at levels

26:40.466 --> 26:42.906 align:middle
above our 2 percent objective.

26:43.276 --> 26:45.126 align:middle
The Committee is
equally committed--

26:45.126 --> 26:46.766 align:middle
this is a symmetric goal--

26:46.766 --> 26:49.396 align:middle
and the Committee
is equally committed

26:49.676 --> 26:52.076 align:middle
to not allowing inflation

26:52.326 --> 26:55.926 align:middle
to persist below our
2 percent objective.

26:56.656 --> 27:00.506 align:middle
Now, I've tried to explain--
and many of my colleagues have

27:00.506 --> 27:03.836 align:middle
as well-- why we have
reasonable confidence

27:04.096 --> 27:06.376 align:middle
that inflation will
move up over time,

27:06.656 --> 27:09.756 align:middle
and the Committee declared
it had reasonable confidence.

27:10.046 --> 27:14.236 align:middle
Nevertheless, that is a
forecast, and we really need

27:14.236 --> 27:18.646 align:middle
to monitor over time
actual inflation performance

27:18.876 --> 27:22.946 align:middle
to make sure that it is
conforming, it is evolving,

27:23.156 --> 27:25.016 align:middle
in the manner that we expect.

27:25.056 --> 27:27.386 align:middle
So it doesn't mean that we need

27:27.446 --> 27:31.966 align:middle
to see inflation reach 2
percent before moving again,

27:32.356 --> 27:36.686 align:middle
but we have expectations for
how inflation will behave.

27:37.046 --> 27:43.136 align:middle
And were we to find that the
underlying theory is not bearing

27:43.136 --> 27:47.096 align:middle
out, that it is not behaving
in the manner that we expect,

27:47.356 --> 27:51.226 align:middle
and that it doesn't look like
the shortfall is transitory

27:51.226 --> 27:54.326 align:middle
and disappearing with
tighter labor markets,

27:54.376 --> 27:56.136 align:middle
that would certainly
give us pause.

27:56.136 --> 28:00.126 align:middle
And we have indicated that
we're reasonably close--

28:00.126 --> 28:02.396 align:middle
not quite there, but
reasonably close--

28:02.396 --> 28:05.276 align:middle
to achieving our maximum
employment objective,

28:05.686 --> 28:08.886 align:middle
but we have a significant
shortfall on inflation.

28:09.186 --> 28:12.446 align:middle
And so we're calling
attention to the importance

28:12.446 --> 28:15.416 align:middle
of verifying our--
that things evolve

28:15.416 --> 28:16.876 align:middle
in line with our forecasts.

28:16.876 --> 28:17.506 align:middle
JON HILSENRATH.

28:17.506 --> 28:20.486 align:middle
Just to follow up, do
you need to see it rise?

28:20.486 --> 28:23.486 align:middle
Not necessarily get to the
2 percent goal, but in order

28:23.486 --> 28:25.046 align:middle
to move again, do you want

28:25.046 --> 28:27.426 align:middle
to see inflation measures
actually moving higher?

28:27.826 --> 28:28.156 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

28:28.156 --> 28:31.366 align:middle
I'm not going to give you a
simple formula for what we need

28:31.366 --> 28:37.026 align:middle
to see on the inflation front
in order to raise rates again.

28:37.326 --> 28:41.476 align:middle
We'll also be looking at the
path of employment as well

28:41.476 --> 28:43.136 align:middle
as the path for inflation.

28:43.536 --> 28:47.346 align:middle
But if incoming data were--

28:47.596 --> 28:51.856 align:middle
led us to call into question
the inflation forecast

28:51.856 --> 28:55.946 align:middle
that we have set out, and
that could be a variety

28:55.946 --> 28:58.386 align:middle
of different kinds of evidence,

28:58.656 --> 29:01.006 align:middle
that would certainly
give the Committee pause.

29:01.006 --> 29:03.866 align:middle
But I don't want to say
there's a simple benchmark.

29:04.316 --> 29:07.246 align:middle
We-- you know, the
Committee expects inflation--

29:07.556 --> 29:11.796 align:middle
over the next year, the median
expectation is for inflation

29:11.836 --> 29:20.136 align:middle
to be running about 1.6 percent,
and-- both core and headline.

29:20.406 --> 29:23.186 align:middle
So we do expect it
to be moving up,

29:23.616 --> 29:27.396 align:middle
but we don't expect
it to reach 2 percent.

29:27.816 --> 29:29.786 align:middle
CRAIG TORRES.

29:34.056 --> 29:36.976 align:middle
Hi, Madam Chair, Craig
Torres from Bloomberg.

29:37.396 --> 29:39.366 align:middle
I'd like to follow
Jon's question.

29:39.556 --> 29:42.986 align:middle
The way the Committee
describes inflation--

29:42.986 --> 29:45.226 align:middle
well, there's this
"transitory" language.

29:45.496 --> 29:50.166 align:middle
I'd like to point out that
oil prices today are at 36,

29:50.166 --> 29:52.466 align:middle
and on June 15, they were $60.

29:52.466 --> 29:55.856 align:middle
So, this "transitory," which
first appeared in the statement

29:56.026 --> 29:59.526 align:middle
in December, I believe, is
lasting a long, long time,

29:59.526 --> 30:02.166 align:middle
maybe longer than many
people's definition

30:02.166 --> 30:03.686 align:middle
of "transitory,"
and it could go on.

30:04.226 --> 30:06.466 align:middle
And, second, I really wonder

30:06.466 --> 30:12.196 align:middle
if the Committee knows
how quickly wage increases

30:12.196 --> 30:15.896 align:middle
or labor market tightness
transfers into higher prices,

30:16.376 --> 30:18.446 align:middle
and that too is also a forecast.

30:18.566 --> 30:21.726 align:middle
So, my question: What
will you be willing to do

30:21.966 --> 30:25.186 align:middle
if you don't see progress
toward 2 percent inflation?

30:25.636 --> 30:29.136 align:middle
We missed the target for
three years, and, you know,

30:29.446 --> 30:31.416 align:middle
what would you be willing to do?

30:31.416 --> 30:35.046 align:middle
And, second, would you
allow inflation to bounce

30:35.046 --> 30:38.166 align:middle
around between 2 and 3 percent
the way you've allowed it

30:38.206 --> 30:41.346 align:middle
to move under 2 percent
over the past several years?

30:41.516 --> 30:42.996 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR YELLEN.

30:43.106 --> 30:45.956 align:middle
First, let me say with
respect to oil prices,

30:46.276 --> 30:50.526 align:middle
I have been surprised by the
further downward movement

30:50.526 --> 30:56.296 align:middle
in oil prices, but we do not
need to see oil prices rebound

30:56.296 --> 30:59.516 align:middle
to higher levels in
order for the impact

30:59.796 --> 31:02.076 align:middle
on inflation to wash out.

31:02.156 --> 31:05.356 align:middle
So all they need
to do is stabilize.

31:05.706 --> 31:08.246 align:middle
I believe there is
some limit below

31:08.246 --> 31:11.426 align:middle
which oil prices are
unlikely to rise.

31:12.016 --> 31:13.736 align:middle
If we look-- to fall.

31:13.736 --> 31:16.676 align:middle
If we look at market
expectations,

31:16.676 --> 31:19.756 align:middle
market expectations
are for stabilization

31:19.756 --> 31:23.006 align:middle
and then some gradual
upward movement.

31:23.286 --> 31:26.476 align:middle
So I certainly grant
that we've had a series

31:26.476 --> 31:29.966 align:middle
of shocks pushing them down,
but we're not looking for them

31:29.966 --> 31:33.556 align:middle
to revert back to the-- to
higher levels that they were

31:33.556 --> 31:36.126 align:middle
at merely to stabilize.

31:37.956 --> 31:39.856 align:middle
We-- I would point
out, you asked me,

31:39.856 --> 31:41.816 align:middle
would we tolerate overshoots.

31:41.976 --> 31:47.776 align:middle
For a number of years between
2004 and 2008, we had a series

31:47.776 --> 31:51.546 align:middle
of increases in oil
prices that for a series

31:51.546 --> 31:54.886 align:middle
of years raised inflation
above--

31:55.156 --> 31:58.506 align:middle
again, we didn't have a 2
percent objective then, but--

31:58.706 --> 32:02.676 align:middle
raised it above 2 percent,
and we judged those increases

32:02.706 --> 32:06.206 align:middle
to be transitory as well
and looked through them.

32:06.666 --> 32:10.036 align:middle
We do monitor inflation
expectations very carefully.

32:10.856 --> 32:12.646 align:middle
If we saw in a meaningful way

32:12.646 --> 32:17.926 align:middle
that inflation expectations
were either moving up in a way

32:17.926 --> 32:20.626 align:middle
that made it-- them
seem unanchored or down,

32:20.926 --> 32:22.766 align:middle
that would be of concern.

32:23.086 --> 32:25.266 align:middle
And we have called attention

32:25.266 --> 32:29.436 align:middle
to some slight downward
movements in survey measures.

32:29.466 --> 32:32.326 align:middle
We are watching that,
but I still judge

32:32.326 --> 32:37.276 align:middle
that inflation expectations
are reasonably well anchored.

32:37.506 --> 32:42.806 align:middle
So, yes, we have tolerated
inflation shortfalls

32:43.116 --> 32:48.016 align:middle
that we thought would disappear
over the medium term just

32:48.016 --> 32:51.036 align:middle
as we did overshoots
of inflation

32:51.036 --> 32:53.806 align:middle
that we also judged
to be transitory.

32:54.216 --> 32:58.356 align:middle
But we do need to monitor
inflation very carefully

32:58.356 --> 33:02.846 align:middle
because if energy prices and
the dollar were to stabilize,

33:02.846 --> 33:07.296 align:middle
import prices, our expectation
is that both headline

33:07.296 --> 33:09.736 align:middle
and core inflation
would move up.

33:10.066 --> 33:14.056 align:middle
And if we failed to see
that occurring in the manner

33:14.056 --> 33:18.376 align:middle
that we expect, of course we
need to take further action

33:18.376 --> 33:22.266 align:middle
to reconsider the outlook and to
put in place appropriate policy.

33:22.266 --> 33:22.696 align:middle
CRAIG TORRES.

33:22.696 --> 33:25.306 align:middle
What would that action
look like?

33:25.926 --> 33:26.296 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

33:26.296 --> 33:28.826 align:middle
Well, you know, if the economy
were disappointing, we--

33:29.206 --> 33:32.896 align:middle
you know, our actions wouldn't
purely be based on inflation,

33:33.116 --> 33:35.836 align:middle
we would also take
employment into account.

33:36.086 --> 33:39.136 align:middle
So I can't give you
a simple answer,

33:39.466 --> 33:42.976 align:middle
but we would pursue a
more accommodative policy

33:43.266 --> 33:45.926 align:middle
because we do-- certainly
are committed

33:45.926 --> 33:48.296 align:middle
to achieving 2 percent
over the medium term.

33:48.856 --> 33:49.676 align:middle
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

33:50.556 --> 33:52.356 align:middle
Binya Appelbaum,
the New York Times.

33:52.596 --> 33:54.416 align:middle
Bill Dudley has talked
about the need for the Fed

33:54.416 --> 33:56.756 align:middle
to adjust policy based
on the responsiveness

33:56.756 --> 33:59.386 align:middle
of financial markets as you
begin to increase rates.

33:59.966 --> 34:01.356 align:middle
You didn't talk about
that today.

34:01.766 --> 34:03.206 align:middle
Is it a point that
you agree with?

34:03.206 --> 34:05.076 align:middle
And, if so, what is it
that you're looking for?

34:05.076 --> 34:07.666 align:middle
How will you judge whether
financial markets are accepting

34:07.666 --> 34:08.936 align:middle
and transmitting these changes?

34:09.676 --> 34:10.176 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

34:10.176 --> 34:12.276 align:middle
Well, there are number
of different channels

34:12.276 --> 34:15.776 align:middle
through which monetary
policy is transmitted

34:15.776 --> 34:17.746 align:middle
to spending decisions.

34:18.116 --> 34:22.396 align:middle
The behavior of longer-term--
longer-term interest rates,

34:22.396 --> 34:24.496 align:middle
short-term interest
rates matter.

34:24.836 --> 34:30.816 align:middle
The value of asset prices
and the exchange rate, also--

34:31.046 --> 34:33.186 align:middle
these are transmission channels.

34:34.166 --> 34:39.536 align:middle
We wouldn't be focused on
short-term financial volatility,

34:40.006 --> 34:45.886 align:middle
but were there unanticipated
changes in financial conditions

34:46.536 --> 34:51.666 align:middle
that were persistent and we
judged to affect the outlook,

34:51.926 --> 34:54.546 align:middle
we would of course have to
take those into account.

34:55.016 --> 34:57.586 align:middle
So we will watch
financial developments,

34:57.586 --> 35:01.886 align:middle
but what we're looking at here
is the longer-term economic

35:01.886 --> 35:05.406 align:middle
outlook: Are we seeing
persistent changes

35:05.766 --> 35:10.746 align:middle
in financial market conditions
that would have a bearing,

35:10.746 --> 35:14.126 align:middle
a significant bearing, on the
outlook that we would need

35:14.126 --> 35:17.316 align:middle
to take account in
formulating appropriate policy?

35:17.596 --> 35:21.236 align:middle
Yes, we would, but it's
not short-term volatility

35:21.236 --> 35:22.006 align:middle
in markets.

35:22.196 --> 35:22.646 align:middle
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

35:22.646 --> 35:23.666 align:middle
In part, what I was
attempting to say is,

35:23.666 --> 35:25.946 align:middle
if you didn't see changes, you
would be concerned and have

35:26.016 --> 35:26.986 align:middle
to move more, quickly.

35:27.306 --> 35:29.666 align:middle
Are you concerned that
if markets don't tighten

35:29.916 --> 35:31.276 align:middle
sufficiently you
may need to do more?

35:31.616 --> 35:32.006 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

35:32.006 --> 35:33.536 align:middle
Well, look, you know, we--

35:34.266 --> 35:37.446 align:middle
this is not an unanticipated
policy move,

35:37.906 --> 35:40.256 align:middle
and we have been trying

35:40.306 --> 35:43.616 align:middle
to explain what our
policy strategy is.

35:43.986 --> 35:47.886 align:middle
So it's not as though I'm
expecting to see a marked,

35:48.236 --> 35:51.446 align:middle
immediate reaction
in financial markets.

35:51.446 --> 35:56.286 align:middle
Expectations about Fed
policy have been built

35:56.286 --> 36:00.106 align:middle
into the structure of
financial market prices,

36:00.506 --> 36:04.396 align:middle
but we obviously will track
carefully the behavior

36:04.636 --> 36:08.816 align:middle
of both short- and longer-term
interest rates, the dollar,

36:08.816 --> 36:12.296 align:middle
and asset prices, and if
they move in persistent

36:12.296 --> 36:16.316 align:middle
and significant ways
that are out of line

36:16.316 --> 36:19.306 align:middle
with the expectations
that we have,

36:19.636 --> 36:21.806 align:middle
then of course we will
take those into account.

36:22.936 --> 36:25.966 align:middle
REBECCA JARVIS.

36:26.186 --> 36:26.726 align:middle
Thank you.

36:26.956 --> 36:28.506 align:middle
Rebecca Jarvis, ABC News.

36:29.176 --> 36:33.516 align:middle
Historically, most economic
expansions fade after this long.

36:33.976 --> 36:37.526 align:middle
How confident are you that
our economy won't slip back

36:37.526 --> 36:39.266 align:middle
into recession in the near term?

36:39.456 --> 36:39.916 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

36:40.046 --> 36:43.406 align:middle
So let me start by saying
that I feel confident

36:43.406 --> 36:47.206 align:middle
about the fundamentals
driving the U.S. economy,

36:47.626 --> 36:51.296 align:middle
the health of U.S. households,
and domestic spending.

36:51.746 --> 36:55.616 align:middle
There are pressures on some
sectors of the economy,

36:55.616 --> 37:01.216 align:middle
particularly manufacturing
and the energy sector,

37:01.216 --> 37:04.596 align:middle
reflecting global
developments and developments

37:04.596 --> 37:07.776 align:middle
in commodity markets
and energy markets,

37:08.106 --> 37:09.756 align:middle
but the underlying health

37:09.756 --> 37:13.736 align:middle
of the U.S. economy I
consider to be quite sound.

37:14.176 --> 37:19.086 align:middle
I think it's a myth that
expansions die of old age.

37:19.086 --> 37:21.326 align:middle
I do not think that
they die of old age.

37:22.246 --> 37:26.666 align:middle
So the fact that this has been
quite a long expansion doesn't

37:26.866 --> 37:31.416 align:middle
lead me to believe that
it's one that has--

37:31.536 --> 37:33.086 align:middle
its days are numbered.

37:33.606 --> 37:37.856 align:middle
But the economy does
get hit by shocks,

37:38.266 --> 37:41.886 align:middle
and there are both positive
shocks and negative shocks.

37:41.886 --> 37:45.226 align:middle
And so there is a
significant odd, you know,

37:45.226 --> 37:49.836 align:middle
probability in any year that the
economy will suffer some shock

37:49.946 --> 37:54.716 align:middle
that we don't know about that
will put it into recession.

37:55.006 --> 37:58.856 align:middle
And so I'm not sure exactly
how high that probability is

37:58.856 --> 38:02.926 align:middle
in any year, but maybe at least
on the order of 10 percent.

38:03.176 --> 38:05.436 align:middle
So, yes, there is
some probability

38:05.726 --> 38:07.126 align:middle
that that could happen,

38:07.126 --> 38:11.216 align:middle
and of course we'd appropriately
respond, but it isn't something

38:11.216 --> 38:14.756 align:middle
that is fated to happen because
we've had a long expansion,

38:14.996 --> 38:19.526 align:middle
and I don't see anything
in the underlying strength

38:19.526 --> 38:22.586 align:middle
of the economy that
would lead me

38:22.586 --> 38:24.416 align:middle
to be concerned about
that outcome.

38:24.786 --> 38:25.776 align:middle
REBECCA JARVIS.

38:25.776 --> 38:28.466 align:middle
In the event of an outcome
like that, in the most negative

38:28.466 --> 38:32.376 align:middle
of scenarios, are there
other policy measures outside

38:32.376 --> 38:35.776 align:middle
of interest rates, outside of
traditional quantitative easing,

38:36.106 --> 38:39.726 align:middle
that you inside of the Fed
have discussed and contemplated

38:39.986 --> 38:42.716 align:middle
for another environment
like something

38:42.716 --> 38:44.566 align:middle
which we saw throughout
the Great Recession?

38:44.806 --> 38:46.126 align:middle
Have you talked about anything

38:46.126 --> 38:48.636 align:middle
that would be more
direct to the economy?

38:48.916 --> 38:49.496 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

38:49.496 --> 38:53.326 align:middle
Well, during the years
in which we were--

38:53.746 --> 38:55.886 align:middle
you know, the economy
was recovering

38:55.886 --> 39:00.366 align:middle
from the Great Recession and we
put further policy and measures

39:00.366 --> 39:05.806 align:middle
in place, we studied our
policy options quite carefully.

39:06.476 --> 39:09.546 align:middle
As you know, communications
policy

39:09.546 --> 39:13.216 align:middle
to affect market
expectations about the path

39:13.216 --> 39:15.976 align:middle
of interest rates
played an important role.

39:16.906 --> 39:18.306 align:middle
That's something we did

39:18.906 --> 39:22.136 align:middle
in conjunction with
asset purchases.

39:22.556 --> 39:26.916 align:middle
Obviously, we lowered our
overnight interest rate

39:26.916 --> 39:28.546 align:middle
effectively to zero.

39:28.936 --> 39:33.486 align:middle
Now, we have seen some
foreign central banks, Europe--

39:33.546 --> 39:38.126 align:middle
the ECB and others-- that have
taken their overnight rate

39:38.126 --> 39:42.586 align:middle
into negative territory,
and that's something that--

39:42.746 --> 39:45.286 align:middle
I don't contemplate that
we will need to do this,

39:45.366 --> 39:48.266 align:middle
but it is something
that we could study.

39:48.266 --> 39:50.446 align:middle
Of course, we have
balance sheet policies,

39:50.696 --> 39:55.666 align:middle
and there might be a
range of direct policies

39:55.826 --> 39:57.206 align:middle
that we could use as well.

39:57.206 --> 40:00.916 align:middle
But this is something that
we have thought about,

40:00.916 --> 40:02.046 align:middle
our range of options.

40:02.766 --> 40:03.436 align:middle
SAM FLEMING.

40:03.906 --> 40:04.716 align:middle
Sam Fleming.

40:05.136 --> 40:07.106 align:middle
Sam Fleming from
the Financial Times.

40:07.106 --> 40:09.186 align:middle
Could I ask about the
balance sheet policy you're

40:09.186 --> 40:10.996 align:middle
adopting today?

40:10.996 --> 40:12.786 align:middle
You've said that you want to
keep a large balance sheet

40:12.786 --> 40:14.996 align:middle
until normalization
is "well under way."

40:14.996 --> 40:17.426 align:middle
Could you explain first
of all why you anticipate

40:17.426 --> 40:18.836 align:middle
that being appropriate?

40:18.836 --> 40:22.126 align:middle
What does "well under way"
mean in terms of normalization?

40:22.546 --> 40:24.856 align:middle
And, longer term, what
are your views on the idea

40:24.856 --> 40:27.366 align:middle
that the Fed might need to
have a larger balance sheet

40:27.416 --> 40:29.616 align:middle
than historically
over the longer term?

40:29.976 --> 40:30.516 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

40:30.566 --> 40:35.676 align:middle
Well, in our normalization
principles, which are in effect,

40:36.126 --> 40:42.606 align:middle
the Committee stated that we
eventually want to operate

40:42.606 --> 40:44.536 align:middle
with a much smaller
balance sheet of--

40:44.656 --> 40:46.146 align:middle
than we have at present.

40:46.646 --> 40:51.386 align:middle
The-- we would reduce the
size of the balance sheet

40:51.506 --> 40:55.816 align:middle
to essentially whatever
size we needed

40:55.816 --> 40:58.786 align:middle
to manage monetary
policy effective--

40:58.786 --> 41:01.416 align:middle
in an effective and
efficient way.

41:01.846 --> 41:06.466 align:middle
Now, a lot has changed since
pre-financial crisis in terms

41:06.466 --> 41:11.026 align:middle
of the financial system,
and we are studying--

41:11.026 --> 41:14.946 align:middle
we are engaging in a
project at this time

41:15.256 --> 41:19.306 align:middle
to consider what our long-run
operating framework should

41:19.376 --> 41:19.956 align:middle
look like.

41:20.266 --> 41:23.406 align:middle
So I can't tell you
exactly what size

41:23.406 --> 41:28.196 align:middle
of balance sheet we will
determine is the best to operate

41:28.196 --> 41:30.426 align:middle
in an efficient and
effective manner.

41:30.576 --> 41:34.796 align:middle
It might be somewhat larger
than the very tiny quantity

41:34.796 --> 41:37.486 align:middle
of reserves that we
had in pre-crisis.

41:37.486 --> 41:38.986 align:middle
We have not determined that.

41:39.216 --> 41:44.526 align:middle
We have also said that we will--
we expect to reduce the size

41:44.526 --> 41:48.616 align:middle
of our balance sheet
over time by cease--

41:48.666 --> 41:52.506 align:middle
diminishing or ceasing
entirely reinvestments.

41:52.506 --> 41:56.776 align:middle
And, beyond that, we haven't
given additional guidance other

41:56.846 --> 42:00.736 align:middle
than to say that the
timing of reductions

42:00.736 --> 42:03.766 align:middle
in reinvestment will
depend on economic

42:03.766 --> 42:05.716 align:middle
and financial conditions.

42:06.096 --> 42:10.246 align:middle
And I suppose the additional
guidance we're giving today,

42:10.246 --> 42:14.586 align:middle
when we say "well under
way," we want to see--

42:14.796 --> 42:17.286 align:middle
as I mentioned, there are
number of considerations,

42:17.286 --> 42:22.266 align:middle
and the Committee has made no
further decisions about this,

42:22.666 --> 42:27.246 align:middle
but as we've discussed, the
factors that will be relevant

42:27.316 --> 42:31.126 align:middle
to the decision-- one
factor that we've talked

42:31.126 --> 42:36.916 align:middle
about is the desirability of
having some scope to respond

42:36.916 --> 42:39.186 align:middle
to an adverse shock
to the economy

42:39.626 --> 42:41.686 align:middle
by lowering the federal
funds rate.

42:41.996 --> 42:47.206 align:middle
And so it would be nice
to have a buffer in terms

42:47.206 --> 42:51.766 align:middle
of having raised the federal
funds rate to a certain extent

42:51.766 --> 42:55.076 align:middle
to give us some meaningful
scope to respond.

42:55.076 --> 42:58.396 align:middle
Now, I don't have in
mind any particular level

42:58.396 --> 42:59.686 align:middle
of the federal funds rate.

42:59.856 --> 43:03.026 align:middle
It would depend on the
entire economic outlook,

43:03.026 --> 43:04.916 align:middle
how robust the economy is.

43:05.266 --> 43:10.056 align:middle
But that is an important
consideration for the Committee,

43:10.056 --> 43:14.546 align:middle
and it means that this is
not something that we expect

43:14.596 --> 43:18.496 align:middle
to be turning to, to cease
reinvestment very quickly.

43:20.176 --> 43:21.156 align:middle
PETER BARNES.

43:22.076 --> 43:23.446 align:middle
Peter Barnes at Fox
Business, ma'am.

43:23.766 --> 43:28.346 align:middle
On financial market conditions,
is there a problem developing

43:28.376 --> 43:29.706 align:middle
in the high-yield sector

43:29.756 --> 43:33.726 align:middle
of the bond market,
the junk bond sector?

43:33.726 --> 43:37.326 align:middle
As you know, last week a mutual
fund, Third Avenue Management,

43:37.626 --> 43:41.546 align:middle
halted redemptions so
that it wouldn't have to--

43:41.546 --> 43:45.566 align:middle
because of a sharp selloff
in these kinds of bonds.

43:45.956 --> 43:48.646 align:middle
Are you concerned at all, and
did you discuss in the meeting,

43:48.976 --> 43:51.946 align:middle
any risk to the financial
system, a systemic risk,

43:51.946 --> 43:55.506 align:middle
because of the conditions
in the junk bond market?

43:55.886 --> 43:57.776 align:middle
And, you know, individual
investors

43:57.776 --> 44:00.636 align:middle
and institutions hold trillions
of dollars of these bonds,

44:00.676 --> 44:03.656 align:middle
which were sold heavily
because of low rates,

44:03.656 --> 44:07.556 align:middle
and did the Fed's low
interest rate policy help lay--

44:07.726 --> 44:09.516 align:middle
sow the seeds for
this development?

44:09.666 --> 44:09.946 align:middle
Thank you.

44:10.476 --> 44:10.846 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

44:10.946 --> 44:12.446 align:middle
Well, risk spreads

44:12.446 --> 44:15.616 align:middle
in the high-yield bond
market have been widening

44:15.616 --> 44:20.396 align:middle
since last year, partly
reflecting falling oil prices--

44:20.786 --> 44:23.036 align:middle
only partly, but partly--

44:23.326 --> 44:26.846 align:middle
and redemptions in high-yield
bond funds have been increasing

44:27.066 --> 44:28.146 align:middle
in recent months.

44:28.486 --> 44:34.396 align:middle
But Third Avenue Focused Credit
Fund was a rather unusual

44:34.396 --> 44:35.946 align:middle
open-end mutual fund.

44:35.946 --> 44:40.936 align:middle
It had very concentrated
positions in especially risky

44:41.196 --> 44:46.126 align:middle
and illiquid bonds, and it had
been facing very significant

44:46.126 --> 44:47.576 align:middle
redemption pressures.

44:47.806 --> 44:53.166 align:middle
My understanding is that the SEC
is in touch with Third Avenue.

44:53.166 --> 44:58.846 align:middle
And, as you probably know, the
SEC has proposed some reforms

44:59.196 --> 45:04.616 align:middle
to address what's a structural
problem of liquidity mismatch

45:04.616 --> 45:06.556 align:middle
in open-end mutual funds.

45:06.986 --> 45:11.066 align:middle
So we continue to believe

45:11.186 --> 45:14.956 align:middle
that financial conditions are
supportive of economic growth.

45:15.466 --> 45:19.406 align:middle
We will be-- we have
been and will continue

45:19.486 --> 45:21.876 align:middle
to track developments

45:21.876 --> 45:25.626 align:middle
in financial markets
very carefully.

45:26.116 --> 45:31.946 align:middle
I would say that I think we have
a far more resilient financial

45:32.146 --> 45:36.756 align:middle
system now than we had prior
to the financial crisis

45:37.066 --> 45:41.316 align:middle
and highly capital[ized]
banks that are well situated

45:41.446 --> 45:44.426 align:middle
to support corporate lending.

45:44.856 --> 45:49.306 align:middle
I'd also point out that many
corporations during these years

45:49.556 --> 45:52.026 align:middle
have reduced their
interest payments

45:52.226 --> 45:54.556 align:middle
and extended their
debt profiles,

45:54.906 --> 45:58.366 align:middle
and I think that should
help to mitigate spillovers,

45:58.366 --> 46:02.116 align:middle
but we will be evaluating
this carefully.

46:03.676 --> 46:11.626 align:middle
YLAN MUI. Hi, Ylan from
the Washington Post.

46:11.806 --> 46:14.706 align:middle
You said earlier that
expansions don't die of old age,

46:14.706 --> 46:16.056 align:middle
but I think the other
half of that is

46:16.056 --> 46:18.956 align:middle
that it's often central banks
that kill them off instead.

46:18.956 --> 46:23.316 align:middle
So I'm wondering how worried
you are about the possibility

46:23.606 --> 46:26.926 align:middle
that the Fed will have to turn
around after hiking rates.

46:27.276 --> 46:28.656 align:middle
Other central banks
that have tried

46:28.656 --> 46:30.316 align:middle
to raise rates have
had to do just that.

46:30.546 --> 46:32.086 align:middle
And how damaging you
think that might be

46:32.086 --> 46:33.036 align:middle
to the Fed's credibility?

46:33.786 --> 46:34.206 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

46:34.236 --> 46:38.796 align:middle
So, when you say that
central banks often kill them,

46:39.316 --> 46:45.206 align:middle
I think the usual reason
that that has been true

46:45.206 --> 46:47.516 align:middle
when that has been true is

46:47.556 --> 46:52.896 align:middle
that central banks have begun
too late to tighten policy,

46:53.306 --> 46:57.336 align:middle
and they've allowed inflation
to get out of control.

46:57.806 --> 47:02.706 align:middle
And at that point, they have had
to tighten policy very abruptly

47:02.706 --> 47:06.646 align:middle
and very substantially,
and it's caused a downturn,

47:07.746 --> 47:10.956 align:middle
and the downturn has
served to lower inflation.

47:11.446 --> 47:14.826 align:middle
So, if you don't mind my
flipping the question on you,

47:15.136 --> 47:20.606 align:middle
I would point out that it
is because we don't want

47:20.606 --> 47:23.626 align:middle
to cause a recession
through that type of dynamic

47:23.876 --> 47:27.066 align:middle
at some future date
that it is prudent

47:27.066 --> 47:29.746 align:middle
to begin early and gradually.

47:30.306 --> 47:33.866 align:middle
Now, it is true that some
central banks have raised rates

47:33.866 --> 47:36.636 align:middle
and later turned around.

47:37.286 --> 47:41.456 align:middle
Not in every case has that
reflected a policy mistake.

47:41.836 --> 47:44.276 align:middle
Economies are subject to shocks.

47:44.596 --> 47:47.536 align:middle
Sometimes when they
have raised rates,

47:47.536 --> 47:49.506 align:middle
it hasn't been the
wrong thing to do,

47:49.846 --> 47:54.476 align:middle
but conditions have changed
in a way that they have had

47:54.476 --> 47:58.516 align:middle
to reverse policy to
respond to shocks.

47:58.836 --> 48:02.786 align:middle
I'm not denying that
there are situations

48:02.786 --> 48:06.146 align:middle
where central banks
have moved too early.

48:06.956 --> 48:10.616 align:middle
We have considered
the risk of that.

48:10.756 --> 48:12.986 align:middle
We have weighed that
risk carefully

48:13.366 --> 48:15.206 align:middle
in making today's decision.

48:15.506 --> 48:17.306 align:middle
I don't believe we'll
have to do it.

48:17.786 --> 48:21.326 align:middle
But, look, you know, as I've--
as the Committee has said,

48:21.606 --> 48:24.586 align:middle
we're watching economic
developments closely,

48:24.936 --> 48:30.586 align:middle
and we will adjust policy
in whatever way is necessary

48:30.666 --> 48:33.066 align:middle
to support the attainment
of our objectives.

48:34.696 --> 48:37.826 align:middle
JIM PUZZANGHERA.

48:39.496 --> 48:41.266 align:middle
Hi, Jim Puzzanghera
from the L.A. Times.

48:41.416 --> 48:43.576 align:middle
You said it's important not
to overblow the significance

48:43.576 --> 48:45.646 align:middle
to this first move, but, as
you know, there's been a lot

48:45.646 --> 48:46.726 align:middle
of attention paid to it.

48:47.186 --> 48:49.126 align:middle
Can you explain to
average Americans what

48:49.126 --> 48:51.396 align:middle
if anything might be
different in the next few weeks

48:51.396 --> 48:55.706 align:middle
or few months for them because
of this interest rate hike?

48:56.906 --> 48:57.146 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

48:57.146 --> 48:59.766 align:middle
So I think the first--
the first thing

48:59.766 --> 49:03.746 align:middle
that Americans should realize is

49:03.996 --> 49:09.906 align:middle
that the Fed's decision
today reflects our confidence

49:10.196 --> 49:17.066 align:middle
in the U.S. economy, that we
believe we have seen substantial

49:17.066 --> 49:19.606 align:middle
improvement in labor
market conditions,

49:20.016 --> 49:26.376 align:middle
and while things may be uneven
across regions of the country

49:26.376 --> 49:30.816 align:middle
and different industrial
sectors, we see an economy

49:30.876 --> 49:35.056 align:middle
that is on a path of
sustainable improvement.

49:35.386 --> 49:39.296 align:middle
So, in thinking about their
labor market prospects

49:39.296 --> 49:42.616 align:middle
and their financial
prospects going forward,

49:42.896 --> 49:47.226 align:middle
I hope they will take
this decision as one

49:47.696 --> 49:51.916 align:middle
that signals the
FOMC's confidence

49:52.006 --> 49:56.386 align:middle
that conditions will
continue to strengthen

49:56.386 --> 49:58.606 align:middle
and job market prospects
will be good.

49:59.046 --> 50:01.696 align:middle
It is a very small move.

50:01.756 --> 50:06.646 align:middle
It will be reflected in some
changes in borrowing rates.

50:07.446 --> 50:09.556 align:middle
Longer-term interest rates--

50:09.556 --> 50:11.836 align:middle
loans that are linked

50:11.866 --> 50:15.426 align:middle
to longer-term interest
rates are unlikely

50:15.486 --> 50:18.376 align:middle
to move-- to move very much.

50:19.616 --> 50:23.706 align:middle
Some-- for example, some
corporate loans are linked

50:23.706 --> 50:26.876 align:middle
to the prime rate,
which is likely to move

50:26.876 --> 50:28.456 align:middle
up with the fed funds rate,

50:28.696 --> 50:30.826 align:middle
and those interest
rates will adjust.

50:31.196 --> 50:34.936 align:middle
There are some consumer
borrowing rates,

50:35.166 --> 50:39.396 align:middle
I think credit card rates, that
are linked to short-term rates

50:39.426 --> 50:41.056 align:middle
that might move up slightly.

50:41.436 --> 50:45.336 align:middle
But, remember, we
have very low rates,

50:45.366 --> 50:47.126 align:middle
and we have made
a very small move.

50:48.396 --> 50:49.326 align:middle
JOHN HELTMAN.

50:51.496 --> 50:53.786 align:middle
Madam Chair, John Heltman
with American Banker.

50:54.236 --> 50:57.796 align:middle
How concerned are you with
interest rate risk in banks now

50:57.796 --> 51:01.826 align:middle
that you've obviously
ended the zero-rate era

51:02.006 --> 51:05.496 align:middle
and began the 0.25
percent-rate era?

51:05.496 --> 51:08.776 align:middle
Is that a factor in
decisions going forward?

51:08.776 --> 51:10.456 align:middle
Is it something that
you're concerned about?

51:10.486 --> 51:11.706 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

51:11.836 --> 51:14.556 align:middle
Interest rate risk
at banks is something

51:14.556 --> 51:17.286 align:middle
that we have been
monitoring very carefully

51:17.356 --> 51:20.206 align:middle
for quite a long time.

51:20.206 --> 51:23.886 align:middle
The community banks and
smaller banking organizations

51:23.886 --> 51:29.176 align:middle
that we work with-- part of our
supervision has been ensuring

51:29.176 --> 51:32.686 align:middle
that they manage appropriately
for interest rate risk.

51:33.106 --> 51:37.336 align:middle
And the larger banking
organizations that are subject

51:37.336 --> 51:40.256 align:middle
to the stress tests
and capital planning--

51:40.636 --> 51:45.426 align:middle
the scenarios that we
have presented in each

51:45.426 --> 51:49.416 align:middle
of the last three years
look at their ability

51:49.466 --> 51:54.646 align:middle
to withstand what would
be much sharper increases

51:54.646 --> 51:57.956 align:middle
in interest rates than we're
envisioning would happen.

51:57.956 --> 52:02.246 align:middle
But we want to make sure that if
there were sharper increases--

52:02.246 --> 52:04.906 align:middle
unlike our expectations--

52:05.176 --> 52:07.696 align:middle
that they would be well
positioned to handle it.

52:07.946 --> 52:10.336 align:middle
And we have concluded that--

52:10.506 --> 52:13.296 align:middle
that their capital
positions are sufficient

52:13.296 --> 52:14.626 align:middle
for them to weather it.

52:14.626 --> 52:16.996 align:middle
So this has been very
much on our minds.

52:17.836 --> 52:18.906 align:middle
LINDSAY DUNSMUIR.

52:19.846 --> 52:21.566 align:middle
Hi, Lindsay Dunsmuir
with Reuters.

52:22.436 --> 52:25.776 align:middle
You keep saying that the things
holding back inflation are

52:25.776 --> 52:29.386 align:middle
transitory, and yet every day
there seems to be new impulses

52:29.386 --> 52:32.646 align:middle
from outside that look that
they could drive prices lower.

52:32.646 --> 52:37.526 align:middle
I guess my question is, if in
a year's time inflation remains

52:37.526 --> 52:39.946 align:middle
where it is today, would you see

52:39.946 --> 52:41.806 align:middle
that as a defeat
for your theory?

52:42.116 --> 52:46.406 align:middle
And is that part of the reason
why the language has been

52:46.406 --> 52:48.056 align:middle
upgraded in the statement
to say that you want

52:48.056 --> 52:51.096 align:middle
to see actual progress
towards your 2 percent target?

52:51.366 --> 52:51.736 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

52:51.736 --> 52:56.226 align:middle
Well, we have said that we will
carefully monitor both actual

52:56.226 --> 52:58.226 align:middle
and expected progress.

52:58.556 --> 53:09.866 align:middle
You know, I think that standard
Fed policy has been to look

53:09.866 --> 53:12.986 align:middle
through shocks that
are transitory.

53:13.826 --> 53:18.186 align:middle
Occasionally, there are
sequences of transitory shocks.

53:18.746 --> 53:24.706 align:middle
We have had some further
declines in energy prices, and,

53:25.316 --> 53:28.736 align:middle
you know, as I said previously,

53:29.056 --> 53:31.376 align:middle
I do expect there
is a bottom to that.

53:31.806 --> 53:36.406 align:middle
I expect we will be seeing it.

53:37.726 --> 53:45.596 align:middle
If we-- if we analyze inflation
data and conclude that, clearly,

53:45.646 --> 53:48.916 align:middle
transitory influences are
holding down inflation,

53:49.326 --> 53:53.986 align:middle
I do not want to say that
we would respond to that.

53:54.506 --> 53:59.686 align:middle
But if we concluded that
there were structural factors

53:59.686 --> 54:03.326 align:middle
or that there were a
problem with our theory

54:03.326 --> 54:06.766 align:middle
or some global deflationary
force

54:07.206 --> 54:12.666 align:middle
that were simply persistently
holding down inflation in a way

54:12.666 --> 54:14.366 align:middle
that was not transitory--

54:14.596 --> 54:21.366 align:middle
and I don't want to attach
any simplistic meaning

54:21.366 --> 54:24.036 align:middle
to what we would need
to see to conclude

54:24.036 --> 54:27.726 align:middle
that in the inflation data,
but if we concluded that,

54:27.906 --> 54:31.886 align:middle
we certainly would take action
to make sure we adjusted policy

54:31.886 --> 54:34.836 align:middle
so that we attain our
2 percent objective.

54:35.106 --> 54:38.526 align:middle
But we would need to feel
that what we were seeing

54:38.526 --> 54:42.306 align:middle
in the data suggested
a sustained departure

54:42.306 --> 54:44.626 align:middle
from our 2 percent objective

54:44.756 --> 54:46.486 align:middle
that we really needed
to address.

54:50.776 --> 54:52.966 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

54:53.176 --> 54:54.606 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

54:54.866 --> 54:57.876 align:middle
There's been a lot of talk
today and a lot of discussion

54:57.876 --> 55:01.506 align:middle
about the downside risks facing
the economy from, you know,

55:01.506 --> 55:04.306 align:middle
the global environment, but
you say in your statement

55:04.306 --> 55:05.666 align:middle
that the risks are
nearly balanced.

55:05.776 --> 55:08.406 align:middle
So should-- could you talk
more about the upside risks

55:08.506 --> 55:09.716 align:middle
that you see to the economy?

55:10.506 --> 55:12.086 align:middle
And, just briefly, the--

55:12.086 --> 55:17.066 align:middle
some Fed officials have been
emphasizing lately the median

55:17.116 --> 55:19.046 align:middle
CPIs from Dallas and Cleveland.

55:19.556 --> 55:21.896 align:middle
How much weight do you
put on those measures?

55:21.896 --> 55:22.796 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

55:22.796 --> 55:26.416 align:middle
Well, starting with the median
CPIs, we look at a range

55:26.416 --> 55:32.916 align:middle
of statistics that bear
on the inflation outlook,

55:33.296 --> 55:38.506 align:middle
and the median CPI has
been somewhat more stable

55:38.716 --> 55:42.046 align:middle
and running closer to
2 percent than the PCE,

55:42.046 --> 55:46.346 align:middle
but there is a systematic gap
between these two measures,

55:46.346 --> 55:51.666 align:middle
and our objective is 2
percent on the PCE price index,

55:51.666 --> 55:54.726 align:middle
and so there's no
simple translation.

55:54.946 --> 55:58.326 align:middle
We do have a PCE
inflation objective.

55:58.646 --> 56:02.146 align:middle
Sorry, let's see, the
other-- the upside risks.

56:02.626 --> 56:06.316 align:middle
So I-- there are upside risks
to the economy, and I think,

56:06.696 --> 56:09.586 align:middle
you know, we tend to focus
on the downside risk.

56:09.586 --> 56:10.716 align:middle
It's right to do so.

56:10.716 --> 56:14.456 align:middle
We want to be careful
about downside risks.

56:14.536 --> 56:22.606 align:middle
But consumers are in much
healthier financial condition.

56:23.136 --> 56:26.176 align:middle
Their income prospects
have improved.

56:27.066 --> 56:30.716 align:middle
We see them buying
a lot of cars.

56:32.046 --> 56:34.906 align:middle
Housing has been
recovering very slowly,

56:35.316 --> 56:41.346 align:middle
but the demographics would
point to considerable upside

56:41.666 --> 56:43.716 align:middle
for residential investment.

56:44.076 --> 56:48.576 align:middle
My mainline forecast is
for gradual recovery,

56:48.816 --> 56:50.876 align:middle
but there is upside risk there.

56:52.226 --> 56:57.396 align:middle
We have seen that the decline

56:57.396 --> 57:00.746 align:middle
in drilling has been
depressing investment spending,

57:01.056 --> 57:02.846 align:middle
but there's upside risk too.

57:03.266 --> 57:05.966 align:middle
The global economy--
we tend to focus--

57:05.966 --> 57:07.506 align:middle
there are many countries

57:07.606 --> 57:11.166 align:middle
that are undergoing very
difficult adjustments

57:11.166 --> 57:15.436 align:middle
or slowing growth, especially
with declining commodity prices.

57:15.826 --> 57:18.046 align:middle
But even recently,
we've seen growth

57:18.046 --> 57:19.816 align:middle
in emerging markets strengthen.

57:20.206 --> 57:24.106 align:middle
And-- so it is not only
downside risks, but, you know,

57:24.106 --> 57:26.246 align:middle
we do pay attention
to downside risks.

57:26.246 --> 57:29.466 align:middle
But the Committee said they
regard the risks overall

57:29.466 --> 57:30.146 align:middle
as balanced.

57:30.706 --> 57:34.896 align:middle
STEVE BECKNER.

57:35.056 --> 57:37.416 align:middle
Chair Yellen, Steve
Beckner of MNI.

57:37.416 --> 57:41.216 align:middle
You have often said that the Fed
is going to be data dependent,

57:41.216 --> 57:44.036 align:middle
but you've also said in
the past that you want

57:44.036 --> 57:46.636 align:middle
to avoid being mechanical.

57:47.346 --> 57:51.366 align:middle
Now, if the data seem
to warrant rate hikes

57:51.366 --> 57:54.726 align:middle
at successive meetings, how
will you avoid the perception--

57:55.456 --> 57:57.806 align:middle
a market perception-- that
you're falling into a pattern

57:57.806 --> 57:59.566 align:middle
or becoming mechanical?

57:59.806 --> 58:00.766 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

58:00.766 --> 58:02.536 align:middle
Well, we will try to avoid that.

58:02.536 --> 58:05.976 align:middle
I do want to emphasize that
while we have said "gradual,"

58:06.366 --> 58:11.546 align:middle
gradual does not mean
mechanical, evenly timed,

58:11.816 --> 58:16.086 align:middle
equally sized interest
rate changes.

58:16.086 --> 58:19.036 align:middle
So that is not what the
Committee means by it.

58:19.426 --> 58:24.726 align:middle
My guess is that the economy
will progress in a manner

58:25.056 --> 58:30.656 align:middle
that is not sufficiently
even that we will decide

58:30.686 --> 58:32.486 align:middle
to make evenly spaced hikes.

58:32.486 --> 58:35.266 align:middle
I recognize we want to
do what's appropriate,

58:35.466 --> 58:37.756 align:middle
and I recognize that's a danger.

58:38.096 --> 58:42.626 align:middle
But I do want to assure you
that we will be data dependent.

58:42.986 --> 58:46.496 align:middle
As the outlook evolves,
we'll respond appropriately.

58:46.926 --> 58:51.786 align:middle
I strongly doubt that it will
mean equally spaced hikes,

58:51.786 --> 58:55.586 align:middle
and it certainly is not the
intention of the Committee

58:55.876 --> 59:01.546 align:middle
to follow any mechanical
formula of that type.

59:01.946 --> 59:03.536 align:middle
BRENDAN GREELEY.

59:04.506 --> 59:05.806 align:middle
Brendan Greeley, Bloomberg.

59:06.496 --> 59:09.416 align:middle
You just mentioned
that it may be possible

59:09.416 --> 59:12.416 align:middle
that structural factors are
holding down inflation as well,

59:12.736 --> 59:14.746 align:middle
and the dynamic that's happening

59:14.746 --> 59:17.726 align:middle
in top-line inflation is
happening in core as well.

59:17.726 --> 59:20.396 align:middle
In September, the median
projection was revised down.

59:20.446 --> 59:23.386 align:middle
Again today, the median
projection was revised down,

59:23.386 --> 59:26.066 align:middle
and this is happening at
other central banks as well.

59:26.316 --> 59:29.526 align:middle
Additionally, markets see much
lower inflation in the future

59:29.526 --> 59:30.956 align:middle
than central bank models do.

59:31.266 --> 59:35.166 align:middle
Within the Fed, how are you
adapting your models to look

59:35.166 --> 59:37.286 align:middle
at what is now-- seems
to be a new reality,

59:37.286 --> 59:38.806 align:middle
and what are you
learning as you do that?

59:39.386 --> 59:39.756 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

59:39.756 --> 59:45.386 align:middle
Well, so the reason-- the
main reason that we revised

59:45.806 --> 59:49.246 align:middle
down our projection
ever so slightly--

59:49.246 --> 59:53.096 align:middle
it's hardly revised
for core inflation--

59:53.276 --> 59:55.976 align:middle
is because we have seen
further appreciation

59:55.976 --> 01:00:00.026 align:middle
of the dollar that's holding
down import prices that spills

01:00:00.026 --> 01:00:02.556 align:middle
over into core inflation.

01:00:02.956 --> 01:00:05.106 align:middle
I mean, my own estimation is

01:00:05.146 --> 01:00:08.256 align:middle
that core inflation
will pick up.

01:00:08.636 --> 01:00:11.466 align:middle
There are various
idiosyncratic factors

01:00:11.506 --> 01:00:13.526 align:middle
that affect core inflation--

01:00:13.526 --> 01:00:17.336 align:middle
for example, nonmarket
price increases,

01:00:17.396 --> 01:00:19.766 align:middle
which are a little bit
hard to understand,

01:00:19.836 --> 01:00:22.146 align:middle
have been running
at a slow pace.

01:00:22.456 --> 01:00:25.556 align:middle
There were factors that have
been affecting inflation

01:00:25.556 --> 01:00:29.116 align:middle
in medical care prices
that may change over time.

01:00:29.116 --> 01:00:32.206 align:middle
So there is some
idiosyncratic factors.

01:00:32.526 --> 01:00:36.456 align:middle
But I personally don't
think we're in a world

01:00:36.526 --> 01:00:40.396 align:middle
where inflation is being
determined in a different way

01:00:40.446 --> 01:00:42.616 align:middle
than it has historically.

01:00:42.886 --> 01:00:49.096 align:middle
I see import prices and energy
prices as holding down headline

01:00:49.136 --> 01:00:52.546 align:middle
but also core, and I do
believe it will pick up.

01:00:52.586 --> 01:00:56.126 align:middle
But as we've said, if
that theory is wrong

01:00:56.126 --> 01:00:59.296 align:middle
and we do not see
inflation is unfolding

01:00:59.296 --> 01:01:01.466 align:middle
in the way the Committee
expects,

01:01:01.726 --> 01:01:04.746 align:middle
we will make adjustments
over time in policy.

01:01:04.746 --> 01:01:05.316 align:middle
BRENDAN GREELEY.

01:01:05.406 --> 01:01:07.126 align:middle
You have a background
as an academic.

01:01:07.126 --> 01:01:09.376 align:middle
Are you looking into
these models

01:01:09.376 --> 01:01:10.856 align:middle
to see whether they
need to be adapted?

01:01:11.236 --> 01:01:11.626 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

01:01:11.626 --> 01:01:14.696 align:middle
I mean, we have many people who
are studying inflation models.

01:01:14.946 --> 01:01:17.556 align:middle
I've, you know, tried to--

01:01:18.216 --> 01:01:22.126 align:middle
let me express some
humility about them.

01:01:22.196 --> 01:01:26.986 align:middle
I do not think that
they're perfect.

01:01:27.486 --> 01:01:31.926 align:middle
Monetary policy is based
on economic forecasts.

01:01:32.476 --> 01:01:35.676 align:middle
There are theories of
how the economy works

01:01:36.046 --> 01:01:40.466 align:middle
that govern many aspects
of economic forecasting.

01:01:41.186 --> 01:01:45.346 align:middle
Whether it's consumer spending
or residential investment

01:01:45.346 --> 01:01:49.666 align:middle
or inflation, the underlying
theories are not perfect,

01:01:50.046 --> 01:01:52.096 align:middle
and they are subject
to uncertainty.

01:01:52.636 --> 01:01:56.416 align:middle
And this is true in all
aspects of forecasting,

01:01:56.966 --> 01:02:01.406 align:middle
which is why we change our
forecasts and our models.

01:02:01.446 --> 01:02:05.216 align:middle
We throw out models that are
persistently not working.

01:02:05.216 --> 01:02:08.636 align:middle
We're always trying to
develop better models.

01:02:09.086 --> 01:02:13.386 align:middle
I'm not aware, I will say, of
a different model of inflation

01:02:13.386 --> 01:02:17.276 align:middle
that would be superior to
the one that we employ.

01:02:17.526 --> 01:02:21.546 align:middle
But we have to verify
that inflation is moving

01:02:21.546 --> 01:02:24.246 align:middle
in the manner that we
expect, and if it's not,

01:02:24.246 --> 01:02:26.676 align:middle
we need to adjust
policy accordingly.

01:02:27.846 --> 01:02:29.016 align:middle
JEREMY TORDJMAN.

01:02:30.196 --> 01:02:33.816 align:middle
Hello, Jeremy Tordjman with
Agence France-Presse, AFP.

01:02:33.816 --> 01:02:35.896 align:middle
Are you concerned about
the negative impact

01:02:35.896 --> 01:02:38.236 align:middle
that your decision could
have on the emerging markets?

01:02:38.656 --> 01:02:41.896 align:middle
Do you fear it could trigger
some financial turbulences

01:02:41.896 --> 01:02:44.046 align:middle
abroad that could
eventually hit the U.S.?

01:02:45.886 --> 01:02:46.506 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

01:02:46.656 --> 01:02:52.906 align:middle
So we are constantly monitoring
foreign economic developments,

01:02:52.906 --> 01:02:56.316 align:middle
including those in
emerging markets.

01:02:57.026 --> 01:03:03.056 align:middle
We understand that in the global
economy, with integrated product

01:03:03.056 --> 01:03:07.106 align:middle
and capital markets, that our
fates are very much linked,

01:03:07.606 --> 01:03:08.946 align:middle
and that the performance

01:03:08.946 --> 01:03:12.876 align:middle
of the U.S. economy has
important spillovers

01:03:12.876 --> 01:03:15.586 align:middle
onto emerging markets
and vice versa.

01:03:17.506 --> 01:03:21.496 align:middle
We have been trying
very carefully--

01:03:21.496 --> 01:03:27.806 align:middle
we've made a commitment to
emerging market policymakers

01:03:27.806 --> 01:03:32.646 align:middle
that we would do our best
to communicate as clearly

01:03:32.646 --> 01:03:38.846 align:middle
as we could about our policy
intentions to avoid spillovers

01:03:39.156 --> 01:03:44.426 align:middle
that might result from abrupt
or unanticipated policy moves.

01:03:44.806 --> 01:03:49.476 align:middle
I think this move has been
expected and well communicated--

01:03:49.566 --> 01:03:51.356 align:middle
at least, I hope that it has.

01:03:51.666 --> 01:03:53.836 align:middle
So I don't think
it's a surprise.

01:03:54.406 --> 01:03:59.056 align:middle
This action takes place in
the context of a U.S. economy

01:03:59.056 --> 01:04:02.226 align:middle
that is doing well and
is a source of strength

01:04:02.606 --> 01:04:04.156 align:middle
to the emerging markets

01:04:04.156 --> 01:04:07.146 align:middle
and other economies
around the globe.

01:04:07.466 --> 01:04:12.586 align:middle
And so-- that is, there
can be negative spillovers

01:04:12.586 --> 01:04:14.616 align:middle
through capital flows,
but remember,

01:04:14.616 --> 01:04:17.056 align:middle
there are also positive
spillovers

01:04:17.056 --> 01:04:19.486 align:middle
from a strong U.S. economy.

01:04:20.016 --> 01:04:27.886 align:middle
My general view is that
many emerging markets are

01:04:27.886 --> 01:04:33.466 align:middle
in a stronger position than they
would have been in the 1990s--

01:04:33.676 --> 01:04:37.926 align:middle
for example, that they have
stronger macroeconomic policies.

01:04:38.426 --> 01:04:39.896 align:middle
They have taken steps

01:04:40.006 --> 01:04:42.366 align:middle
to strengthen their
financial systems

01:04:42.576 --> 01:04:44.926 align:middle
and are better positioned
to deal with this.

01:04:45.326 --> 01:04:49.126 align:middle
On the other hand, there
are vulnerabilities there,

01:04:49.126 --> 01:04:52.726 align:middle
and there are countries that
have been badly affected

01:04:52.726 --> 01:04:55.426 align:middle
by declining commodity prices,

01:04:55.716 --> 01:04:58.046 align:middle
so we will monitor
this very carefully.

01:04:58.046 --> 01:05:05.366 align:middle
But we have taken care to avoid
unnecessary negative spillovers.

01:05:06.446 --> 01:05:08.266 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

01:05:08.266 --> 01:05:09.936 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

01:05:10.716 --> 01:05:13.226 align:middle
Wage growth is not
quite where you would

01:05:13.226 --> 01:05:14.036 align:middle
like it to be-- CHAIR YELLEN.

01:05:14.036 --> 01:05:14.376 align:middle
I'm sorry, what?

01:05:14.376 --> 01:05:15.366 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

01:05:15.366 --> 01:05:17.846 align:middle
Wage growth is not quite
where you would like it to be.

01:05:18.136 --> 01:05:20.696 align:middle
How much is that going to play
into your thinking next year

01:05:20.746 --> 01:05:22.846 align:middle
as you're trying
to decide whether

01:05:22.886 --> 01:05:25.176 align:middle
to raise interest
rates more and when?

01:05:26.156 --> 01:05:26.926 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

01:05:26.926 --> 01:05:31.016 align:middle
So my expectation is that in
a strengthening labor market

01:05:31.126 --> 01:05:35.646 align:middle
that we would see faster wage
growth, and I believe there is,

01:05:35.966 --> 01:05:41.306 align:middle
with the 2 percent inflation
objective, space for wage growth

01:05:41.426 --> 01:05:43.206 align:middle
to be higher than it's been.

01:05:43.636 --> 01:05:49.036 align:middle
We may be seeing some incipient
signs of faster wage growth.

01:05:49.106 --> 01:05:52.556 align:middle
We've seen a pickup in
measures of hourly compensation

01:05:52.836 --> 01:05:56.106 align:middle
and some slight firming
in recent months

01:05:56.106 --> 01:05:58.056 align:middle
in average hourly earnings.

01:05:58.056 --> 01:06:01.476 align:middle
I hesitate to say that
this is a firm trend.

01:06:01.476 --> 01:06:04.116 align:middle
We've been disappointed
in the past.

01:06:04.906 --> 01:06:08.766 align:middle
Wage growth is not-- there are
many factors that affect it--

01:06:08.766 --> 01:06:13.306 align:middle
it's not definitive in any
sense in determining our policy,

01:06:13.636 --> 01:06:16.566 align:middle
but it does have a bearing
on the inflation outlook.

01:06:16.566 --> 01:06:17.926 align:middle
It also has a bearing

01:06:17.926 --> 01:06:22.416 align:middle
on assessing how much slack
there is in the labor market,

01:06:22.416 --> 01:06:25.696 align:middle
and I think a number of
my colleagues looking

01:06:25.696 --> 01:06:29.686 align:middle
at the slow pace of
wage growth have--

01:06:30.246 --> 01:06:32.306 align:middle
you've seen that their estimates

01:06:32.306 --> 01:06:35.036 align:middle
of the longer-run normal
unemployment rate have

01:06:35.116 --> 01:06:35.786 align:middle
come down.

01:06:35.786 --> 01:06:38.496 align:middle
And I think that is
one of the factors

01:06:38.496 --> 01:06:41.576 align:middle
that has prompted
those adjustments.

01:06:41.576 --> 01:06:46.176 align:middle
So, you know, it
does affect views

01:06:46.176 --> 01:06:50.286 align:middle
about just how much slack
there is in the labor market

01:06:50.736 --> 01:07:01.476 align:middle
and the inflation outlook.

